CAT = "caairInspect"; SEG = "INS"
Q = [
("A vehicle with air brakes does not comply with the Highway Traffic Act and regulations. What must happen?",
 "It must not be driven on any road", ["It may finish the day's route", "It may be driven to its next stop", "It may be driven if it is empty"],
 "When a vehicle fails to comply, the driver, operator and owner must ensure it is not driven on any road or highway."),
("Penalties for operating a vehicle that does not comply with the Act can range from:",
 "A fine to impoundment", ["A warning to a fine", "A fine to a road test", "A demerit point to a fine"],
 "The handbook says penalties may range from a fine to vehicle detainment or impoundment."),
("Who can be charged when a vehicle with a defective air brake system is operated?",
 "The driver and the operator", ["Only the vehicle operator", "Only the vehicle owner", "Only the driver at the time"],
 "Operating a vehicle with defects, or failing to do the required inspections, can result in charges against the driver and/or operator."),
("Why park on a level surface to inspect an air brake system?",
 "Some steps need the parking brakes off", ["The gauges read wrong on a slope", "Drain valves only work when level", "Air leaks are louder on a slope"],
 "Some inspection steps are done with the parking brakes released, and a level surface reduces the chance of the vehicle moving unexpectedly."),
("When must wheel chocks or blocks be used during an inspection?",
 "Leaving the seat, parking brakes off", ["Only when inspecting a trailer", "Only on a steep grade", "Only after draining the tanks"],
 "The handbook says chocks or blocks must be used whenever a test or inspection requires the driver to leave the seat with the parking brakes released."),
("By law, to whom must a driver report a defect in the air brake system?",
 "The vehicle operator", ["A DriveTest centre", "The local police", "The brake manufacturer"],
 "Drivers are required by law to report any air brake defects to the vehicle operator."),
("You see that a brake lining does not touch the drum when the brakes are applied. What does this show?",
 "A malfunctioning brake", ["A normal cool drum", "A newly adjusted brake", "A brake with good ABS"],
 "The lining must press against the drum when applied and be clear of it when released. Lining that does not contact the drum when applied indicates a malfunctioning brake."),
("A brake lining has grease on it from a leaking wheel seal. The lining is:",
 "Defective", ["Fine once wiped", "Fine if thick enough", "A concern in rain only"],
 "Grease or oil on the lining causes abnormal brake behaviour. The handbook says lining contaminated with grease or oil is defective."),
("A brake chamber's size number is based on what?",
 "Diaphragm area in square inches", ["Clamp width in millimetres", "Pushrod length in inches", "Air port size in millimetres"],
 "The size of an air brake chamber is based on the area of its diaphragm in square inches."),
("On a steering axle, why must the brake chambers at each end be the same size?",
 "Uneven braking can affect steering", ["Different sizes leak more air", "The governor needs them matched", "The ABS sensors need them matched"],
 "Matching sizes keep brake forces balanced on each end of an axle, which matters most on a steering axle, where imbalance can affect the steering."),
("When should a brake chamber be checked for air leaks?",
 "Spring brakes off, service brakes on", ["Spring brakes on, service brakes off", "With the system fully drained", "With the trailer unhooked"],
 "Chambers must be checked with air in them: spring brakes released and service brakes applied. Leaks can usually be heard."),
("A brake chamber has small, round vent holes made by its manufacturer. What does this mean?",
 "Nothing, they are normal", ["The chamber is defective", "It must be replaced soon", "It is a long-stroke chamber"],
 "Manufactured vent holes are not a concern. Holes or cracks from impact or other damage mean the chamber is defective."),
("A slack adjuster's effective length is the distance between:",
 "Camshaft centre and clevis pin", ["Chamber clamp and pushrod end", "Brake drum and dust shield", "Clevis pin and brake chamber"],
 "Effective length is from the centre of the camshaft to the clevis pin. Using a different pushrod hole changes it."),
("Why must the slack adjusters at both ends of a steering axle have the same effective length?",
 "To keep braking balanced", ["To keep stroke under the limit", "To fit the same pushrod", "To match the drum width"],
 "Slack adjusters act as levers that multiply chamber force, so unequal effective lengths on one axle give unbalanced braking."),
("A hose's outer cover is worn through to the reinforcing fabric, but no leak can be heard yet. The hose is:",
 "Defective now", ["Fine until it leaks", "Fine if it is taped", "Fine on a trailer"],
 "When damage extends into the reinforcing or inner layer, the air line is defective. Damaged or worn lines are a concern even before a leak appears."),
("An air line was repaired with an ordinary hardware fitting not made for air brakes. This is:",
 "Prohibited", ["Fine if it does not leak", "Fine for one trip", "Fine on low-pressure lines"],
 "Air brake systems need fittings specifically designed and approved for that use. Improper fittings or improper repairs are prohibited."),
("How should you check that an air tank is securely mounted?",
 "Look at it and try to move it", ["Drain it and listen for leaks", "Tap it and listen for rust", "Check its pressure on the gauge"],
 "Check both by looking and by attempting to move the tank, and check the brackets and hardware. Unusual movement suggests an insecure tank or bracket."),
("An air compressor should be inspected with the engine:",
 "Stopped", ["Idling", "At 600 to 900 rpm", "At cut-out pressure"],
 "The handbook says air compressors must always be inspected with the engine stopped."),
("How should a good compressor drive belt respond to firm hand pressure?",
 "It gives slightly", ["It does not move at all", "It moves several inches", "It slips on the pulley"],
 "The belt should give slightly under firm hand pressure. Excessive movement means it is too loose. A loose, cut or frayed belt is defective."),
("Which brake drum condition must a driver look for during an inspection?",
 "Cracks or breaks", ["Light surface rust", "Shiny wear marks", "Brake dust build-up"],
 "Drivers must inspect drums and rotors for damage, cracks or breaks. A cracked or broken drum or rotor is a defect."),
]
